      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "78-83",
      "detail": "5th in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The season has thinned to one last afternoon - no pennant chase, no October theater, just a club finishing the book with the lights still on. <strong>Toronto fell 5-1 to the Reds on Saturday</strong>, leaving it at 78-83 and 5th in the AL East, a flat record that says as much about the year as any sermon could. Trey Yesavage gave them a brief, tidy look - two innings, three strikeouts - and John Klein's debut pushed the season's roster count to a franchise-record 64 players, proof this was a team patched together from need and hope. Now comes Max Scherzer, 42 years old in spirit if not yet in ink, taking the ball in the finale with his future undecided. <strong>Watch Scherzer, Yesavage, and the young pieces getting their last audition - then watch the offseason priorities: offense, depth, and a cleaner plan.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated September 27, 2026"
      },
